WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.840
At a high level, Android is a mobile operating system built on a Linux foundation.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.780
That foundation shapes users, processes, files, permissions, and isolation.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:20.560
Android adds its own application framework, services, security model, device management features, Google services in many deployments, and vendor-specific customization.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:27.660
For investigators, the architecture explains why evidence may be stored in different places and why access can vary by device.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.000
Apps operate within a permission and sandboxing model.

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:36.660
Each application is generally isolated from other applications, with its own data areas and permissions.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.920
This matters because artifacts are often tied to a specific app package, version, account, and permission set.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:53.660
A messaging app, browser, map app, or corporate client may store useful local data, but another app should not normally be able to read that data directly.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.160
Storage is layered.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:03.940
Internal storage can include app-private directories, shared media areas, downloads, documents, caches, system data, and databases.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.920
Some devices or environments may also include removable storage.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:18.620
Notifications and logs may contain useful context, but availability depends on Android version, settings, collection method, and whether the data has been overwritten or protected.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.720
Google services and original equipment manufacturer customizations add another layer.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:33.700
A device may use Google accounts, backup, cloud sync, Play services, device management, vendor apps, and customized system features.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.400
Two Android devices can behave differently even when they appear similar to a user.

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:46.460
Version, patch level, vendor configuration, encryption state, and management controls all affect forensic access and interpretation.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.960
The practical takeaway is that Android artifacts should always be interpreted in architectural context.

00:01:52.180 --> 00:02:01.700
Ask which app or system component created the data, where it was stored, what permissions or sync features were involved, and whether the version or device vendor changes the expected behavior.
